# 6. FEATURES, BEHAVIOR AND ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA

6.1 Trace
- Native scrolling only. No scroll-jacking, no scroll snapping that traps users.
- A rail (desktop) or top progress bar with the current stratum label (mobile) shows depth; items are links that jump to strata.
- Each stratum has a shareable URL (route or hash) and Back works correctly.
- Scroll-linked line drawing uses CSS scroll-driven animations when `CSS.supports('animation-timeline: scroll()')`; otherwise IntersectionObserver.
- Every stratum visualization has a static, fully rendered fallback state.
- Acceptance: all six strata readable with animation disabled; no layout shift when the trace line draws; keyboard users reach every annotation.

6.2 Atlas
- Renders every node and edge from the content model. Layout is deterministic (precomputed or seeded) and stable between loads.
- Encoding: stratum by position or region and color; evidence status by shape or fill pattern plus text label.
- Selecting a node opens a panel: label, status, summary, evidence link, related nodes each with their edge reason.
- Filters: evidence status and stratum; simple text search.
- Keyboard: Tab enters the graph, arrow keys move between related nodes, Enter selects, Escape clears. An equivalent accessible list or tree is the primary screen-reader representation; the canvas or SVG is decorative to assistive technology.
- Any dragging has a single-pointer non-drag alternative.
- Mobile (below 768px): no full-graph view. Show filter chips, a list, and a focused view of the selected node with at most 8 neighbors.
- Acceptance: contrast at least 3:1 for graphics and 4.5:1 for text; touch targets at least 44px on touch devices (never below 24px); no hover-only information; at 360px nothing overlaps or requires horizontal scroll.

6.3 Lab
- Verify that the packet routing simulation and the cache eviction workbench exist in the source; improve them, do not replace them. If either does not exist, report it and do not fabricate.
- Shared simulation chrome: "SIMULATION" label, Models / Does not model note, play/pause/step/reset, speed control, seed or scenario selector, readable state log (also exposed through a throttled aria-live region).
- Separate pure logic from rendering. Review the algorithms for correctness (for example eviction order for each policy offered; hop counts and path selection for routing). Add unit tests for the pure logic if a test runner exists or a minimal one (such as Vitest) is justified.
- Pause animation when offscreen or the tab is hidden. Cap canvas DPR at 2. Under reduced motion: no autoplay, step-driven.
- Acceptance: deterministic for a given seed; fully operable by keyboard; usable at 360px with controls below the canvas.

6.4 Receipt
- Generated from the experience data. Columns: role, organization, period. Include certification and contact.
- Contact uses only mechanisms that already exist (mailto, LinkedIn, GitHub, existing form). Add a copy-email button with a clipboard fallback. Do not add a backend.
- Link to a résumé file only if one already exists in the repo or public folder. Do not generate or invent one.
- A print stylesheet produces a clean one-to-two page résumé from this section.
- Entry animation (lines printing sequentially) is under 1.5 seconds total, skippable, and disabled under reduced motion. No gimmick totals or joke lines that misrepresent anything.

6.5 Plain view
- Text-first, light "paper" theme, linear document of all content from the same data. Fully functional with minimal JavaScript. Also the print source.

6.6 Crawlability and sharing
- Ensure meaningful content exists without running JavaScript: at minimum a static fallback in the HTML, plus JSON-LD Person data using only verified fields and existing links, canonical, correct title and meta, and verified OG/Twitter tags (check that the OG image exists and renders). Consider build-time prerendering only if the audit shows it is low risk. Do not migrate frameworks.

6.7 Case file (conditional)
